<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> <div><p>Hegel describes <em>Original history</em> as history written by people like Thucydides and Caesar. What is the defining characteristic of this method?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">The historian imposes a philosophical framework on events to reveal their rational structure.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">The historian synthesizes the work of earlier chroniclers into a comprehensive universal account.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">The historian writes about events they witnessed or participated in directly, sharing the spirit of the age they describe.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">The historian relies primarily on oral tradition and legend as sources.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> <div><p>Hegel is critical of <em>Reflective history</em> despite acknowledging its broader scope. What is his main objection to it?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">It relies on too few primary sources and is therefore factually unreliable.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">It tends to judge and interpret past events through the lens of the historian's own age rather than grasping them on their own terms.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">It is too abstract and ignores the concrete details that make history interesting.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">It focuses too narrowly on political history and neglects art, religion, and culture.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> <div><p>Hegel describes his own approach as <em>Philosophical history</em>. Which of the following best captures what this method aims to do?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">Evaluate historical figures according to universal moral standards independent of their time and place.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">Construct a comprehensive factual record of all human civilizations from the earliest times to the present.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">Identify the rational principle already at work in history rather than imposing an idea on it from outside.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">Argue that history is essentially random and that philosophical analysis cannot reveal any underlying pattern.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> <div><p>Hegel states that “Reason governs the world, and has consequently governed its history.” What does he mean by this claim?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">Individual historical actors always behave rationally and make decisions based on logical analysis.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">History has an intelligible rational structure and direction — it is not random, but moves toward a specific end.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">History is guided directly by God, whose rational plan determines every event.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">Science and philosophy have gradually replaced superstition as the governing force in human affairs.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> <div><p>When Hegel refers to <em>Geist</em> (Spirit or Mind) in the <em>Philosophy of History</em>, what does he mean?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">The soul of an individual human being, which Hegel believes survives death.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">The Christian God, understood as a personal being who intervenes in historical events.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">A self-developing rational principle — something like the collective mind of humanity — whose essential nature is freedom and which realizes itself through history.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">The abstract laws of logic, which Hegel believes govern both thought and physical reality.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> <div><p>According to Hegel, what is the essential nature of Spirit, and what does the history of the world represent?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">Spirit is essentially rational order; world history represents the spread of scientific knowledge.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">Spirit is essentially freedom; world history is the process of Spirit progressively realizing what it essentially is.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">Spirit is essentially moral virtue; world history is the gradual victory of goodness over evil.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">Spirit is essentially power; world history is the record of its accumulation by successive civilizations.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> <div><p>The German word <em>Aufhebung</em> is central to Hegel's dialectic. It is translated as “sublation.” Which of the following best describes what <em>Aufhebung</em> involves?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">The complete elimination of the thesis by the antithesis, with nothing preserved.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">A compromise that splits the difference between two opposing positions.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">A process that simultaneously cancels, preserves, and elevates — producing something genuinely new while retaining what was true in both the thesis and antithesis.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">The return of a historical period to its original form after a temporary deviation.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> <div><p>According to Hegel's dialectical view of history, what is the primary driver of historical change?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">External pressures such as war, climate, and geography.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">The decisions of great rulers and statesmen acting on rational principles.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">Internal contradictions within a historical position or form of life that generate opposition and force development.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">The gradual accumulation of technological improvements that transform material conditions.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> <div><p>Both Socrates and Hegel use the word “dialectic,” but they mean different things by it. Which of the following correctly distinguishes the two uses?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">Socratic dialectic operates through conversation to expose contradictions in held beliefs; Hegelian dialectic describes how ideas and history develop through contradiction on a larger scale.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">Socratic dialectic is concerned with history; Hegelian dialectic is concerned with individual moral improvement.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">Socratic dialectic produces synthesis; Hegelian dialectic produces aporia (puzzlement).</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">The two uses are identical; Hegel simply applied Socrates' method to a broader historical canvas.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> <div><p>Hegel argues that history involves what he calls the “cunning of Reason.” What does he mean by this?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">Reason disguises itself as emotion and passion in order to manipulate individuals into irrational behavior.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Reason works through the private passions and ambitions of individuals — who do not know they are serving a larger purpose — to realize its own ends in history.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Philosophers secretly guide political events by advising rulers who publicly claim to act on intuition alone.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">Reason conceals its conclusions in order to test whether the observer is capable of grasping them independently.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q11c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">11</span> <div><p>Hegel uses Napoleon as his primary example of a world-historical individual. According to Hegel, what distinguishes such figures from ordinary historical actors?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11a" id="q11a">
        <label for="q11a">They possess unusual moral virtue and consciously subordinate their personal interests to the common good.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11b" id="q11b">
        <label for="q11b">They understand Hegel's philosophy and deliberately act to advance the development of Spirit.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11c" id="q11c">
        <label for="q11c">Their personal ambitions happen to coincide with what the development of Spirit requires at that moment; they intuit what history needs without necessarily understanding it philosophically.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11d" id="q11d">
        <label for="q11d">They are chosen by God to fulfill a specific mission and are protected from personal failure until that mission is complete.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q12b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">12</span> <div><p>Hegel observes that world-historical individuals — Caesar, Napoleon — tend to meet tragic ends once their historical task is accomplished. How does he explain this?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12a" id="q12a">
        <label for="q12a">Their moral failings catch up with them; history ultimately rewards virtue and punishes wrongdoing.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12b" id="q12b">
        <label for="q12b">Once the historical principle they embodied has been established, they are no longer needed; they are instruments of Spirit and are discarded when the work is done.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12c" id="q12c">
        <label for="q12c">Their success breeds complacency, which leads to strategic errors and eventual military or political defeat.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12d" id="q12d">
        <label for="q12d">Hegel does not actually discuss the fates of world-historical individuals; this is a later interpretation.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q13b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">13</span> <div><p>Hegel divides world history into three stages based on how widely freedom is recognized. Which of the following correctly describes all three stages in order?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13a" id="q13a">
        <label for="q13a">No one is free; some are free; all are free.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13b" id="q13b">
        <label for="q13b">One is free (the despot); some are free (Greek and Roman citizens); all are free (the modern rational state).</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13c" id="q13c">
        <label for="q13c">Individuals are free; communities are free; the state is free.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13d" id="q13d">
        <label for="q13d">Freedom is natural; freedom is legal; freedom is moral.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q14c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">14</span> <div><p>Hegel writes that “the Owl of Minerva spreads its wings only with the falling of the dusk.” What does this image mean in the context of his philosophy of history?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14a" id="q14a">
        <label for="q14a">Philosophy should work in secret, away from the scrutiny of public opinion.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14b" id="q14b">
        <label for="q14b">Wisdom comes most naturally to those who have lived long, difficult lives.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14c" id="q14c">
        <label for="q14c">Philosophy can only comprehend a historical epoch once it has run its course; genuine understanding of one's own time comes only in retrospect.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14d" id="q14d">
        <label for="q14d">The end of history is near; philosophy's task is almost complete.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q15b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">15</span> <div><p>The course has traced a philosophical arc from Hume through Kant to Hegel. Which of the following best describes the progression of this debate?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15a" id="q15a">
        <label for="q15a">Hume argued that knowledge comes from reason alone; Kant corrected him by showing that experience is also necessary; Hegel reconciled the two by arguing that all knowledge is historical.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15b" id="q15b">
        <label for="q15b">Hume showed that reason cannot reach beyond experience; Kant responded that the mind actively structures experience using its own categories, but cannot know things-in-themselves; Hegel rejected the mind/world divide entirely, arguing that reason and reality are aspects of a single developing process.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15c" id="q15c">
        <label for="q15c">Hume established empiricism as the only valid method; Kant extended empiricism to include moral philosophy; Hegel extended it further to cover world history.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15d" id="q15d">
        <label for="q15d">Hume argued for skepticism about causation; Kant accepted this skepticism; Hegel rejected it by arguing that causation can be directly observed in historical events.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  
  
</div>
